okay so you've seen the tone of the  video and you're thinking he's gone  eh DS finally got to me cracked but this  is an absolutely true story that I've  never really told anybody before now in  order for you to understand it I first  need to give you some backstory Wells  growing up we had two cats Henry and  snowy and I loved them to absolute bits  and because I love them so much I got  into something of a nighttime routine  which I did every night from the age of  about six or seven to the age of  eighteen every night before I could go  to sleep I would do several things  firstly I would kiss my teddy goodnight  obviously then I'd go and find Henry and  kiss him goodnight and I tell him about  my day so what I've done in school and I  think fun idol and my friends it was  almost like writing in a diary and if he  wasn't sleeping in my room which often  did then I would go out into the house  in my pants and find him incidentally  finding a jet black cat in a completely  dark house is not easy  then after I'd done that with Henry I  would do exactly the same thing to snow  but I would tell him about what I was  doing the following day  so any homework that I had Julian and he  tests that were coming up basically my  schedule for the whole day I'm not crazy  now this is all fine and dandy when we  all lived in the same house but when I  was about 10 we moved a couple of miles  down the road and only Henry came with  us  basically snowy you kind of adopted some  next-door neighbors because a really  nice garden and he'd spend pretty much  all this time sunbathing in their garden  so when we moved the decision was kind  of made that they would adopt him and  only one cat would come with us  naturally this threw something of a  spanner in the works of my nighttime  schedule as before I would kiss teddy  good night and I guess Henry good night  Ellen on a day  but with snowy I took what I thought was  the only logical option  I found the window in the new house  which approximately pointed towards the  direction of our old house and I would  talk to the window as if it was snowy  again most of the time just in my pants  are awful I'm not  I'm not crazy now that's perfectly  rational and same behavior continued up  until I was 18 and I was doing my  a-levels and it was at this point that  my parents just by chance bumped into  our old neighbors and they unfortunately  told us that snowy had died a couple of  months before just as old age so for the  six months or so leading up to that  chance meeting I had been talking to a  window pretending it was my cat whereas  actually I've been talking to a window  pretending it was a cat which was it was  dead six months that was an awkward  final time saying goodnight bye Jane so  it turns out you're Ted however I  genuinely really believe that this  process of talking to my two cats one of  him just happened to be dead some of the  time was a really big part of my  academic success at school and getting  into Oxford and it stems from the fact  that I think learning is a three-step  process step one preconditioning  yourself to learn so this is the  environment that you are going to be  working in your attitude towards the  work what you've eaten any preparations  that you've put into working in that  session step two actually learning new  material and then step three processing  that material so categorizing in filing  the new information that you have away  in your brain such that you you know  know where it sits in relation to  everything else that you know now each  of those steps are very important in  that own right you have to be in the  right frame of mind to work you have to  pay attention and concentrate when  you're asked taking in new information  and you do need to be able to categorize  what you've learned you need to be able  to access it again and know where it  sits in relation to all the other  knowledge you have in your life however  there's a fourth step which is arguably  the most important of all and it means  that learning actually works in a cycle  one you precondition yourself to learn  to you absorb new information three you  process that information and then four  you evaluate your learning experience  and that evaluation then feeds back into  how you precondition ourselves to learn  in future by evaluating what I mean is  reviewing the information that you've  just learned so going over the bits that  you understand and maybe the bits that  you don't understand quite so well  almost to teach  information again to yourself and then  use the information that you've kind of  glean from that introspection to inform  how you learn in the future so  identifying processes that you learn  very quickly so you know that you don't  need to allocate too much time to  learning new processes like those but  also recognizing processes that you took  awhile to learn and then saying well I  need to allocate more time to those in  the future what I was doing by talking  to my cats was exactly this I was  reviewing the information that I had  learned in that day and explaining it to  Henry and then taking that review of the  information and using it to inform my  plans for the next day so when I talked  to snowy about what I kind of scheduled  for the next day saying to myself well  actually this biddin go quite so well I  think tomorrow maybe I should look over  that again now I'm not necessarily  suggesting that you talk to cats though  if you had cats do that really good  listeners but that you should consider  daily review and evaluation of the work  that you've done in that day school or  at uni and that doesn't have to be cat  it could be writing in a diary or just  setting five to ten minutes aside before  you go to bed to think about what you've  done that day when I was in Oxford I  actually kept a revision diary which  allowed me to write down what I done in  that day and then it actually allowed me  to track ahead so I knew that a week or  a month in a in the future I'd review  the material that I'd done on that given  day which is an excellent tip by the way  but it also at the end of every day  allows me to review what I done and then  use that review to inform what I was  going to do the next day this is just  something that I personally benefited  from a lot particularly when I was in  school and I thought that maybe you guys  would too if you did like this topic and  the style of video but I highly highly  recommend you check out Tom Frank's  channel college info geek which is full  of so many videos about study tips and  improving our productivity and he's a  lot better at this than I am if you did  like this video which I appreciate is a  little bit different for this channel  then do give it a thumbs up so I know  that you guys like this kind of content  and also comment down there if you have  revision tips that you've personally  found particularly useful and successful  then do leave them down there and  I love it nothing more than if we could  learn from each other as a community of  people who are engaged and passionate  about learning so shout it put them down  there thanks very much for watching and  I will see you next time  you
 

